"start_time":"2026-06-22T22:38:05.549648","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":[]},{"cell_type":"raw","id":"b8800035","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.053198,"end_time":"2026-06-22T22:38:05.705315","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-06-22T22:38:05.652117","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"654eaaf7","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.051206,"end_time":"2026-06-22T22:38:05.811202","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-06-22T22:38:05.759996","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Chart Summary:\n","\n","This section compares IHT and Universal Credit (UC) at Local Authority District (LAD) level after aggregating the UC data from OA to LAD.\n","\n","There is a **clear negative correlation** , this suggests that LADs with higher rate of Universal Credit Households tend to have lower estimated IHT per person.\n","\n","This supports the expected pattern that IHT is concentrated in more affluent areas, while Universal Credit Households are more common in less affluent areas.\n","\n","However, many areas have **zero estimated IHT** (data sparsity issue), especially at areas with high Universal Credit Household rates.\n","\n","Best to remove these values and check again, to see if the trend is still there.\n","\n","\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":52,"id":"0084ba81","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-22T22:38:05.915472Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-22T22:38:05.913772Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-22T22:38:06.305471Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-22T22:38:06.303205Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.447506,"end_time":"2026-06-22T22:38:06.30864","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-06-22T22:38:05.861134","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","output_type":"stream","text":["\u001b[1m\u001b[22m`geom_smooth()` using formula = 'y ~ x'\n"]},{"data":{"image/png":"iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAA0gAAANICAIAAAByhViMAAAABmJLR0QA/wD/AP+gvaeTAAAg\nAElEQVR4nOzdZ1wU19oA8Ge2