 Summary:\n","\n","By removing the high value outliers, a negative correlation can be seen in areas with high rate of Households on Universal Credit and the estimated average prices by property type (UK House Price Index)."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"c22cff15","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.069667,"end_time":"2026-06-22T22:38:10.215437","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-06-22T22:38:10.14577","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Section Summary:\n","\n","This section investigates local deprivation by utilizing Universal Credit household rates as a proxy for low-income pressure. By cross-referencing this metric with wealth indicators such as Inheritance Tax (IHT) data, Land Registry sale prices, and the UK House Price Index, this EDA section showed a consistent negative correlation between areas with higher Universal Credit claim rates and those with higher property values or IHT per capita. The findings, which employ log-scaling to mitigate the influence of extreme outliers and data sparsity, suggest that higher concentrations of Universal Credit households are reliably associated with lower relative wealth markers (especially property values) across the analyzed Local Authority Districts.\n"]}],"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"display_name":"R","language":"R","name":"ir"},"language_info":{"codemirror_mode":"r","file_extension":".r","mimetype":"text/x-r-source","name":"R","pygments_lexer":"r","version":"4.4.0"},"papermill":{"default_parameters":{},"duration":391.16286,"end_time":"2026-06-22T22:38:10.907785","environment_variables":{},"exception":null,"input_path":"__notebook__.ipynb","output_path":"__notebook__.ipynb","parameters":{},"start_time":"2026-06-22T22:31:39.744925","version":"2.6.0"}},"nbformat":4,"nbformat_minor":5}